CLASS:: PanWFS_Cardioid
SUMMARY:: Equidistant Linear Wave Field Synthesis Panner for Directional Sound Sources in Cartesian Space
CATEGORIES:: UGens>Multichannel>Panners>PanWFS
RELATED:: Classes/PanWFS_Monopole, Classes/PanWFS_Calibrate

DESCRIPTION::

PanWFS_Cardioid renders a single virtual point source for directional sound sources (like human head) over a straight line array of loudspeakers using 2½-D Wave Field Synthesis (WFS). It outputs signals for n channels of audio, applying the per-speaker delay and amplitude derived from the Rayleigh/Huygens formulation.

This UGen is designed for both able to create non-focused (behind the speaker) and focused (front of the speaker) sound sources.

Note: You might encounter with allocation error due to memory with very large arrays. In this case please please increase the memory by [s.options.memSize = 1024 * 2048], 2Gb is minumum recommended. Then the limitation is the CPU. Please be aware of CPU usage and it is recommended to so adjust your server numWireBufs (to 128) and blockSize (to 256).

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD::ar

ARGUMENT::input

The input signal. (WARNING - 1 channel audio only).

ARGUMENT::virutal_x

Position of the sound source on the X-axis in meters. 0.0 defines point is the middle of the speaker array (-) values describes the direction towards left side of the speaker and (+) values to the right.

ARGUMENT::virutal_z

Position of the sound source on the Z-axis in meters. 0.0 defines point is the middle of the speaker array. (+) values are for the sources behind the speaker (non-focused) and (-) value in front of the speaker (focused).

ARGUMENT::yaw

The direction of the sound source in radiants. A sound source with -90° (-pi/2) of yaw looks straight towards the audiance, 0° towards stage right (+x) and 180° (pi) towards stage left (-x).

ARGUMENT::number_of_speakers
Number of speakes in the speaker array.(WARNING - Even number of speakers only).

ARGUMENT::speaker_distance
Speaker distance between the each speakers in meters.

ARGUMENT::zRef

Reference line depth Δz0 (m); pick near audience.

ARGUMENT::roomTemp

Room's temperature plays a role in the speed of sound. It is highly recommended to have a thermometer with temperature and humidity reading. You can also implement a sensor with OSC for accurate reading.

ARGUMENT::humidity

Relative humidity in %. (eg. for 41% relative humidity enter 41)

ARGUMENT::groundElevation

Rooms elevation above mean sea level in meters (use negative for below sea level).
Internally converted to local ambient barometric via the standard-atmosphere barometric formula.
If unknown, set to 0 (sea level); valid for about 0–11,000 m with small error.

Local ambient barometric pressure is in pascals (At sea level = 101325 Pa).

ARGUMENT::max_delay
It is the buffer size of the delay. Increase it with caution.

ARGUMENT::lagTime
Position smoothing time in seconds. When the source moves, the per-loudspeaker delays and gains glide to their new values over this interval (one-pole smoothing) so motion does not click or zipper. Larger values are smoother but more sluggish; smaller values track movement more tightly. 0 disables smoothing (instant, may click). Typical: 0.02.

ARGUMENT::kFactor
System calibration constant K, in dB. It is the sound pressure level produced at 1 m for a virtual source fed a 0 dBFS-RMS signal through THIS array — the single number that ties the digital signal to physical SPL, folding in the audio interface, amplifier, loudspeaker sensitivity and the array summation. Measure it once with a sound-level meter using LINK::Classes/PanWFS_Calibrate::, which prints the value.

Together with CODE::maxSpl:: it scales the source so its loudness is expressed in real dB SPL. The applied gain is CODE::10 ** ((maxSpl - kFactor) / 20)::.

Default CODE::0:: means "uncalibrated": with CODE::maxSpl:: also 0 the gain is unity and no SPL scaling is applied.

ARGUMENT::maxSpl
The intended level of this source, in dB SPL at 1 m, for a full-scale (0 dBFS) signal — i.e. the loudest level the source reaches. Choose it from the source's real-world loudness: whisper ~30, conversation ~60, raised voice ~75, trumpet (ff) ~90, power tool ~100, car horn ~110.

Because the applied gain is CODE::10 ** ((maxSpl - kFactor) / 20)::, a normalised whisper and a normalised siren are separated purely by their CODE::maxSpl::, while the dynamics within each signal are preserved.

WARNING::
CODE::kFactor:: and CODE::maxSpl:: are meant to be set together, AFTER calibration. With the defaults (both CODE::0::) the gain is unity (SPL scaling off). Setting CODE::maxSpl:: while CODE::kFactor:: is still CODE::0:: applies a gain of CODE::10 ** (maxSpl / 20):: — for CODE::maxSpl = 90:: that is roughly +90 dB, which clips hard and can damage loudspeakers. Always obtain CODE::kFactor:: from LINK::Classes/PanWFS_Calibrate:: first.
::

EXAMPLES::

CODE::
// 1. Calibrate once (see PanWFS_Calibrate). Play the tone in WFS render and read a
//    sound-level meter on-axis at the reference line (meter 2 m in front of the array).
c = { PanWFS_Calibrate.ar(numChans: 8, speaker_distance: 0.127, sourceDepth: 1.0, refRms: -20) }.play;
~k = PanWFS_Calibrate.k(measuredSpl: 78, refRms: -20, sourceDepth: 1.0, micDistance: 2.0);
c.free;

// 2. A directional source facing the audience (yaw -pi/2), rendered at ~90 dB SPL.
SynthDef(\WFScard, { |out = 0, virtual_x = 0.0, virtual_z = 2.0, yaw = (-pi/2)|
    var sig = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.01, 0.2), Impulse.kr(2));
    sig = PanWFS_Cardioid.ar(sig, virtual_x, virtual_z, yaw, 8, 0.127, 2,
        kFactor: ~k, maxSpl: 90);
    Out.ar(out, sig);
}).add;
::
